      "text": "{\"persuasion\":\"UNPERSUADED\",\"support_scope\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"The entry's distinguishing claim is that the biblical 'new moon' (hodesh) is the FULL moon, standing in the east gate as the month's beginning. Of the texts printed with the entry, every one except Psalm 81:3 is phase-neutral: 1 Sam 20, Isa 66:23, Amos 8:5, Ezek 46, Col 2:16 establish that a new-moon day existed and was observed, not which phase marked it. The whole weight of the identification therefore rests on reading Ps 81:3's asyndetic pair hodesh/keseh as an identity rather than as two occasions. The standard counter-reading — month begins at conjunction/first crescent; keseh names the distinct mid-month full-moon feast day — explains the same verse at least as well and integrates better with evidence the book itself supplies: the chapter cites Lev 23:5 placing the Passover sacrifice on the 14th and the chag on the 15th, and Ps 81:3 expressly sets keseh (full moon, which the book agrees is keseh's meaning) on 'the day of our chag.' A 15th-day chag coinciding with the full moon is exactly what a conjunction/crescent month-start yields, and is impossible under a full-moon start, where the 15th is dark; the book escapes only by broadening chag to include new-moon feasts, an admittedly non-standard move. The 'east gate' assertion likewise fails as support: Ezek 46:1 opens the same gate on every sabbath, when the moon is at arbitrary phases, so the gate-opening cannot function as a lunar-phase signal; the counter (gate opened to honor holy convocation days in an east-facing temple) explains both openings uniformly. Job 10:17 is, by the book's own concession, about Job's accusers, and Ps 89:36-37 is phase-neutral throne simile. The counter-interpretation accounts for all printed texts with no novel steps, while the book's case requires an identity reading of contested parallelism, a redefined chag, a phase-signal mechanic that breaks on sabbaths, and chapter-level supports (pictographs, zodiac-tribe tables, a conjectured Hebrew Revelation) that are methodologically weak. The core identification is therefore not the best explanation of the supplied evidence.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The biblical 'new moon' (hodesh, 'renewed moon') is the phase that begins the month, and that phase is the FULL moon — the light that enters the sky's east gate and serves as the sign of the appointed times.\",\"book_secondary_elaborations\":[\"The rendering 'renewed moon' for hodesh (from the root hadash, to renew)\",\"'The full moon in the east gate' — the claim that Ezekiel 46's east-gate opening on the new-moon day corresponds to the full moon rising in the east at nightfall\",\"'Sign of the appointed times' — the moon as the appointed marker of the moedim\"],\"unsupported_glossary_assertions\":[\"The identification of the month-starting phase as the full moon — carried only by a contested identity reading of Ps 81:3's asyndetic parallelism, against the two-occasion reading that coheres with the 15th-day chag (Lev 23:5-6, cited in the book's own chapter) falling at keseh/full moon\",\"'The full moon in the east gate' — Ezek 46:1 opens the identical gate on every sabbath regardless of lunar phase, so the gate-opening cannot be a lunar-phase signal and does not support a full-moon month start\"],\"chapter_only_objections\":[\"Pictographic letter-by-letter etymology of het-dalet-shin is not a recognized method of Hebrew lexicography and cannot bear evidential weight\",\"The tribe-to-zodiac encampment table is speculative and rests on post-biblical sources, not the canon the chapter claims to restrict itself to\",\"The appeal to a conjectured 'original Hebrew' of Revelation 12 and 21 is text-critically unsupported\",\"Genesis 15's 'horror of great darkness' is a visionary dread during deep sleep, not an astronomical report about moonlight on the 15th; the 'selfsame night' parallel therefore does not establish a dark 15th\",\"The crucifixion argument concedes Passover falls on the 14th-15th; ancient testimony and Lev 23 chronology place that feast at the full moon, which contradicts rather than supports a full-moon month start; the three-hour darkness was supernatural on any calendar\",\"1 Samuel 20's advance certainty is equally explained by counting a 29/30-day alternation or calculated conjunction; the text is silent about observation method\",\"Sirach 43's plērōma passage describes the moon's whole cycle and does not state that the month begins at fullness\",\"Ecclesiastes 12 is a poem about aging; the youth-as-brightness analogy is reversible and proves nothing about the calendar\",\"The Amos 8:9/eclipse argument treats a supernatural sign as needing natural lunar preconditions, which its own 'supernatural intervention' premise removes\"],\"strongest_counter_interpretation\":\"The 'new moon' is the day beginning the month at the lunar conjunction or first visible crescent — the moon's renewal from darkness — observed as a recurring holy day paired with the sabbath (1 Sam 20; Amos 8:5; Isa 66:23; Ezek 46; Col 2:16). Keseh in Ps 81:3 names the distinct mid-month full-moon feast day: the psalm commands the trumpet at the month's head (hodesh, cf. Num 10:10) and at the full moon (keseh), the 15th-day pilgrimage chag (Lev 23:5-6; cf. Prov 7:20, where keseh is a fixed mid-month date). The east gate of Ezek 46 opens on sabbaths and new moons alike as a liturgical honor to holy days in an east-facing sanctuary, with no lunar-phase mechanics intended.\",\"counter_core_identification\":\"New moon = the conjunction/first-crescent day that begins the month; the full moon (keseh) is a different, mid-month marker on which the great feasts of the 15th fall.\",\"counter_relation_to_book_core\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"material_core_denial\":\"The month does not begin at the full moon: hodesh denotes the moon's renewal at or just after conjunction, and keseh — the full moon — marks the mid-month feast day (the 15th), so the two words in Ps 81:3 name two occasions, not one.\",\"counter_case\":[\"Ps 81:3 ties keseh (full moon) to 'the day of our chag'; Scripture's chagim (Unleavened Bread, Sukkot) fall on the 15th (Lev 23:5-6, quoted in the book's own chapter), and a 15th-day feast coincides with the full moon only if the month begins at conjunction/crescent — making the book's pivotal verse direct evidence for the standard calendar\",\"Hebrew asyndetic parallelism routinely lists related but distinct items; the absence of a waw does not establish identity, and the very translations the book cites (NKJV, ESV, NASB) render the verse as two items, not one\",\"Ezek 46:1 opens the same east gate on every sabbath, when the moon may be at any phase; the gate-opening is a holy-day honor, not a phase signal, so it gives no support to 'the full moon in the east gate'\",\"Prov 7:20 uses keseh as a fixed, known mid-month date of return — consistent with keseh as a distinct calendar point rather than a synonym for the month's first day\",\"The recurring pairing 'new moon and sabbath' (Amos 8:5; Isa 66:23; Ezek 46:3; Col 2:16) treats hodesh as a class of holy day distinct from the mid-month chag, matching the counter's two-marker structure\",\"All remaining printed texts (1 Sam 20; Ps 89:36-37; Job 10:17; Col 2:16) are phase-neutral and are explained by the counter without any additional apparatus\"],\"book_explanatory_advantages\":[\"The identity reading takes Ps 81:3's asyndeton at maximal face value, giving one unified event rather than two\",\"The renewal semantics of hadash (restore to wholeness in Ezek 36:26, Ps 51:10, etc.) map suggestively onto the moon's return to visual completeness\",\"The full moon uniquely satisfies a strong reading of Gen 1:16's lesser light 'governing the night' all night with the stars\",\"The full moon rising in the east at dusk gives a picturesque correspondence to Ezekiel's opened east gate on the new-moon day\"],\"counter_explanatory_advantages\":[\"Explains Ps 81:3 while preserving the plain 15th-day chag calendar of Lev 23 that the book's own chapter cites — the full moon lands on the feast of the 15th exactly when the month starts at conjunction/crescent\",\"Explains the sabbath opening of Ezekiel's east gate uniformly, which the phase-signal reading cannot\",\"Requires no redefinition of chag, no identity claim between two different nouns, no pictographic etymology, and no reconstructed Hebrew Revelation\",\"Coheres with keseh's cognates (which the book concedes mean full moon) functioning as a named mid-month date (Prov 7:20)\",\"Explains the 'new moon and sabbath' pairing as a class of holy days distinct from the mid-month pilgrimage feasts\"],\"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\":[],\"comparative_winner\":\"COUNTER\",\"decisive_sources\":[\"Psalm 81:3 (hodesh/keseh structure)\",\"Leviticus 23:5-6 as cited in the book's own crucifixion argument (chag on the 15th)\",\"Ezekiel 46:1-3 (identical gate opening on sabbaths, defeating the phase-signal mechanic)\",\"Proverbs 7:20 (keseh as a fixed mid-month date)\",\"Amos 8:5 and Colossians 2:16 (new moon paired with sabbath as a distinct holy-day class)\"],\"unresolved_objections\":[\"The waw-less parallelism of Ps 81:3 remains a genuine grammatical oddity that the two-occasion reading must treat as elliptical\",\"Keseh's precise referent in Ps 81:3 (Tishri 15 specifically, or full moons generally) is not settled by the supplied texts\",\"1 Samuel 20's confident advance dating sits slightly more comfortably with any calculated or highly predictable marker than with pure crescent-sighting, though it does not select the full moon\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
